{
  "dom_id": "step-e4289",
  "name": "Example: Expressing a Position Vector in Component Form",
  "problem": "Find the position vector of $A( -1,2 )$ in terms of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252323/assets/269326f4375dfad6d05e4468f4831012bde4a57bc85fbdfc7082cc466d3522dd.png)\n\nRemember that in general, if a point $A$ has coordinates $(a_x,a_y),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,2),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =(-1)\\cdot i+2\\cdot j \\\\  & =-i+2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
